<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phnom Penh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Phnom Penh is a city at the meeting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> and Tonlé Sap rivers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>. It became a royal capital after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> was abandoned in the 15th century and has been the capital of Cambodia since 1865 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-of-cambodia">King Norodom</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> emptied the city in April 1975, and it was repopulated after the Vietnamese invasion of 1979.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phnom-penh.jpg" alt="Phnom Penh" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Looppty · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phnom_Penh_skyline_from_TK_district_Dec_2024.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Phnom Penh lies in the south-central region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, on the banks of the Tonlé Sap, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> and the Bassac rivers, and is fully surrounded by Kandal province. It is a typical flood plain, and flooding in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> season is a problem when the river sometimes overflows its banks. The city takes its name from Wat Phnom, the hill temple, and from Lady Penh, its founder.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Excavations at Choeung Ek, in the south of the city, uncovered one of the largest kiln pottery centres in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, which produced ceremonial vessels from the 5th to the 13th century, and suggest settlement from the 5th century. According to a tale first recorded a century after it is said to have taken place, a woman named Penh found a floating koki tree in the river in 1372. Inside it were four Buddha statues and one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, and she raised a hill with a shrine to house them, later known as Wat Phnom.</p><p>King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ponhea-yat">Ponhea Yat</a> moved the capital here from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-thom">Angkor Thom</a> after Siam captured and destroyed it. Later kings abandoned Phnom Penh because of fighting between royal pretenders and set up their capitals at places such as Pursat, Longvek and Oudong. Japanese immigrants settled on the outskirts of the city in the 17th century, and a Portuguese community carried on commercial and religious activity there. It became the capital again in 1865 and was the national capital under French colonial rule. After independence it was modernised, and earned the nickname the Pearl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> for its French colonial, New Khmer and Art Deco architecture.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a> thousands of refugees fled to the city from the fighting, and by 1975 its population was two to three million. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> cut off supplies for more than a year before the city fell on 17 April 1975, and then forcibly evacuated all its residents. The city faced persecution, forced labour and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>, and remained largely uninhabited until Vietnam-backed forces took it in 1979, when people began to return. Reconstruction followed with foreign investment and aid.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Phnom Penh is the capital and most populous city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> and the seat of its monarchy at the Royal Palace. It is an autonomous municipality with a status equal to that of the provinces. The official language is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a>, and English and French are also used. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> population was 2,129,371 in 2019. Its Water Festival marks the reversing of the flow of the Tonlé Sap River, and the city holds buildings of the New Khmer Architecture of the 1950s and 1960s, associated with the architect Vann Molyvann.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Phnom Penh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cambodia and Laos remained monarchies under the kings at <b>Phnom Penh</b> and Luang Phrabang, supervised by French residents, while much of Laos was administered directly.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The court gradually moved south-east to the region of <b>Phnom Penh</b>, closer to the Mekong trade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Royal Khmer Armed Forces (Forces Armées Royales Khmères, FARK) were equipped under a military aid agreement negotiated with the United States in 1955, and a United States Military Assistance Advisory Group in <b>Phnom Penh</b> supervised the deliveries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-cambodia-1953-1970">Kingdom of Cambodia (1953-1970) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After repeated attacks by the Khmer Rouge regime of Democratic Kampuchea on border villages, Vietnamese forces invaded Cambodia on 25 December 1978, took <b>Phnom Penh</b> on 7 January 1979 and installed the People’s Republic of Kampuchea under Heng Samrin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Socialist Republic of Vietnam · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Khmer Rouge, a Maoist movement under Pol Pot, abolished money and private property and emptied <b>Phnom Penh</b>, and its Killing Fields caused the deaths of 2,700,000 people by mass murder and genocide.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>A Thai edition of 45 volumes followed under Rama VII (1925–35), a Khmer one from <b>Phnom Penh</b> (1931–69), the Sixth Council edition at Rangoon (1954–56, 40 volumes in Burmese script) and a Sinhalese Buddha Jayanti edition of 58 volumes (from 1957).</blockquote><a href="/work/pali-canon">Pali Canon · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
